next-env.d.ts
e2e/.auth/
test-results/
playwright-report/

# Agent tooling — editor-local state, not project source.
#
# `.claude/` is this machine's Claude Code state: `settings.local.json` is a
# per-machine permission allowlist, and an installed skill set vendors a
# `skills/` tree fetched from GitHub. `.agents/` is where that tree actually
# lives and `skills-lock.json` is its lockfile. All of it is `node_modules` by
# another name: nothing here imports it, nothing here builds without it, and it
# belongs to whoever is driving the editor rather than to Fairfame. A lockfile
# is normally committed so a vendored tree can be rebuilt — that rule is for
# dependencies the *project* needs, and committing one for packages the build
# never touches would oblige every clone to reason about them.
#
# **The whole `.claude/` directory is ignored, not only its local half** (12 Aug
# 2026). Previously just `settings.local.json` and `skills/` were, to leave the
# door open for a committable `.claude/settings.json` of shared permissions and
# commands. No such file was ever written, and the cost of holding the door open
# is that anything the tooling invents next shows up untracked in `git status`
# and has to be judged one file at a time. Add a `!.claude/settings.json`
# exception the day the team agrees a shared one — that is a single line, and it
# records the sharing as deliberate rather than as an oversight.
.claude/
.agents/
skills-lock.json

# Editor-local configuration.
#
# `.vscode/` here holds one personal MCP server pointing at a Sentry endpoint —
# a preference about somebody's editor, not a fact about the project. Committed
# `.vscode/extensions.json` is a reasonable thing for a team to want later; add
# a `!` exception for it rather than removing this line.
.vscode/
.idea/

# Sentry Config File
.env.sentry-build-plugin
